    myths: [
      { myth: 'If my partner looks healthy, they cannot have HIV.', fact: 'A person living with HIV can look completely healthy for 8-10 years without treatment — there are no visible symptoms during the chronic phase. The only way to know is to test. In South Africa, 1 in 5 adults aged 15-49 is HIV-positive. Many do not know their status. Appearance tells you nothing about HIV status.' },
      { myth: 'You can get HIV from sharing food, hugging, or using the same toilet.', fact: 'HIV is transmitted only through specific body fluids: blood, semen, vaginal fluid, rectal fluid, and breast milk. It cannot survive outside the body for more than a few minutes. You cannot get HIV from sharing meals, shaking hands, hugging, kissing, sharing a toilet, swimming pools, or mosquito bites. These myths drive stigma and prevent people from testing and seeking treatment.' },
      { myth: 'HIV is a death sentence.', fact: 'With modern treatment (TLD — one pill daily), a person diagnosed with HIV today has a near-normal life expectancy. South Africa\'s ARV programme is the world\'s largest, with over 5.7 million people on treatment. Viral suppression rates exceed 90% for patients who stay on treatment. An HIV-positive person with an undetectable viral load cannot transmit the virus to sexual partners — this is known as U=U (Undetectable = Untransmittable) and is supported by multiple clinical trials.' },
    ],
    provincialNotes: '<strong>KwaZulu-Natal</strong> has the highest HIV prevalence (27% of adults aged 15-49) and runs the most testing volume — uMgungundlovu and eThekwini districts alone test over 2 million people annually. Community-based testing at taxi ranks, churches, and traditional events has been particularly effective here. <strong>Gauteng</strong> has the largest network of fixed testing sites (over 500 facilities) and mobile testing units deployed in Johannesburg CBD, Tshwane, and Ekurhuleni. PrEP uptake is highest in Gauteng, driven by NGO-supported clinics in Hillbrow and Sandton. <strong>Western Cape</strong> achieves the highest linkage-to-care rate (88% of people testing positive start ARVs within 7 days), largely through efficient same-day initiation protocols at community health centres. Self-test kit distribution is most active in the Western Cape through the Desmond Tutu HIV Foundation. <strong>Mpumalanga</strong> has the second-highest prevalence (24%) after KZN and runs large-scale VMMC campaigns — over 400,000 men circumcised since 2012. Testing uptake among young men (15-24) remains the biggest gap. <strong>Eastern Cape</strong> has lower prevalence (19%) but weaker health infrastructure — testing in rural OR Tambo and Alfred Nzo districts depends heavily on mobile units from NGOs like Right to Care. <strong>Limpopo</strong> has the lowest provincial prevalence (14%) but pockets of very high prevalence in Mopani and Vhembe districts, concentrated among migrant populations from Zimbabwe and Mozambique. <strong>Free State</strong> has strong mining-sector testing programmes (mines test employees annually) but community-based testing coverage is thin outside Bloemfontein. <strong>North West</strong> faces challenges with cross-border testing for Batswana nationals and mine workers — Rustenburg district has some of the highest male prevalence rates due to mining migration. <strong>Northern Cape</strong> has the lowest absolute testing volume due to population size, but facility-based testing coverage is adequate — the challenge is reaching farm workers and isolated communities in the Karoo.',
